# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AURIX web — API and admin portal
#
# Copy to `web/.env.local` for local development. On Vercel these are set in
# Project → Settings → Environment Variables instead; `.env.local` is never
# deployed and must never be committed.
#
#   cp web/.env.example web/.env.local
#   cd web && npm install && npm run dev
#
# Nothing here is ever sent to a client. The Flutter app knows only the base
# URL of this deployment and reaches MongoDB exclusively through it. The one
# exception is any variable prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_, which Next.js inlines into
# the browser bundle — there are none below, and none should be added without
# first checking the value is genuinely public.
#
# Derived from `server/.env.example`. Variables that existed there and are
# deliberately absent here are listed at the end, with the reason.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---- MongoDB (required) ---------------------------------------------------
# The Atlas connection string. Replace <USERNAME> and <PASSWORD> with the
# database user's credentials from Atlas -> Database Access.
#
# If the password contains any of  : / ? # [ ] @  it must be percent-encoded,
# or the driver parses the string wrongly and reports a misleading host error.
#
# ## PREFER THE NON-SRV FORM, shown first below
#
# `mongodb+srv://` is not an address — it is an instruction to look up a DNS
# SRV record and connect to whatever hosts it names. That costs a DNS round
# trip on **every cold start**, which serverless has a great many of, and it
# fails outright on networks that answer ordinary A records while refusing SRV
# (corporate DNS, a VPN capturing port 53, some routers). The symptom is
#
#     querySrv ECONNREFUSED _mongodb._tcp.<cluster>.mongodb.net
#
# which the driver reports and which therefore reads like a database fault. It
# is not: it happens before a byte reaches Atlas. The machine this was ported
# on is one of those networks.
#
# The non-SRV form lists the shards directly, so there is no lookup to fail and
# no round trip to pay. Get it from Atlas: Connect -> Drivers -> set the driver
# version to "Node.js 2.2.12 or later".
#
# Note `ssl=true` is explicit here. `mongodb+srv://` implies TLS; `mongodb://`
# does not, and omitting it is how a connection silently stops being encrypted.
MONGODB_URI=mongodb://<USERNAME>:<PASSWORD>@ac-xxxxxxx-shard-00-00.at589pu.mongodb.net:27017,ac-xxxxxxx-shard-00-01.at589pu.mongodb.net:27017,ac-xxxxxxx-shard-00-02.at589pu.mongodb.net:27017/?ssl=true&replicaSet=atlas-xxxxxx-shard-0&authSource=admin&retryWrites=true&w=majority

# The SRV form still works where the network permits it. If you use it and hit
# the error above, either switch to the form above or set DNS_SERVERS below.
# MONGODB_URI=mongodb+srv://<USERNAME>:<PASSWORD>@cluster0.at589pu.mongodb.net/admin?retryWrites=true&w=majority

# Resolvers for the SRV lookup, used only when MONGODB_URI is the `+srv` form
# and the machine's own resolver refuses SRV. Affects `dns.resolve*` and not
# `dns.lookup`, so ordinary outbound connections are untouched.
#
# Leave empty on Vercel — its resolver handles SRV correctly — and leave it
# empty everywhere if you took the non-SRV URI above, which needs no lookup.
# DNS_SERVERS=1.1.1.1,8.8.8.8

# Which database inside the cluster holds AURIX's collections. Deliberately not
# `admin`, even though the URI ends in `/admin` — that path component only
# selects the authentication database.
MONGODB_DB=aurix

# ---- Tokens (required) ----------------------------------------------------
# Sign the JWTs this deployment issues. Use long random values:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))"
#
# Changing either one signs every existing session out, which is the correct
# response to a suspected leak.
JWT_SECRET=
JWT_REFRESH_SECRET=

# How long an access token lives. Short by design: the refresh token keeps a
# user signed in, and a short access token limits the damage of one being
# captured.
ACCESS_TOKEN_TTL=30m

# How long a device stays signed in without re-entering a password.
REFRESH_TOKEN_DAYS=60

# Lifetime of a password-reset or email-verification link, in minutes.
ACTION_TOKEN_MINUTES=60

# ---- HTTP -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Comma-separated origins allowed to call this API from a browser. Leave empty
# in development to reflect whatever origin asks; SET IT IN PRODUCTION.
# Native mobile builds are unaffected — CORS is a browser mechanism.
# CORS_ORIGINS=https://app.example.com,https://aurix-api.vercel.app

# ---- First administrator --------------------------------------------------
# The one account granted admin rights when it registers. Every administrator
# after that is promoted through the API by an existing one. Leave empty once
# the first admin exists.
BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_EMAIL=

# ---- Uploads --------------------------------------------------------------
# Caps enforced before the bytes are read.
#
# NOTE THE FONT CAP. Vercel refuses any request body over 4.5 MB before the
# function runs, so the server's old 4 MB limit could be exceeded by a
# legitimate 4 MB font once multipart overhead is added — the upload would fail
# with a platform error instead of this API's `payload_too_large`. 3.5 MB keeps
# the failure inside the application, where it can be explained.
MAX_LOGO_BYTES=2097152
MAX_FONT_BYTES=3670016

# ---- Mail (optional) ------------------------------------------------------
# Without SMTP this deployment logs reset and verification tokens instead of
# mailing them, and — outside production only — returns them in the response so
# the flow is testable. A production deployment with no SMTP cannot deliver a
# password reset at all; it fails closed rather than leaking the token.
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_SECURE=false
# SMTP_USER=
# SMTP_PASS=
# MAIL_FROM=AURIX <no-reply@aurix.app>

# Where reset and verification links point. Without it the email carries the
# raw code for the user to paste into the app instead.
# PUBLIC_APP_URL=https://app.example.com

# ---- Logging --------------------------------------------------------------
# debug | info | warn | error
LOG_LEVEL=info

# ===========================================================================
# Phone sign-in
# ===========================================================================
# REQUIRES AN SMS PROVIDER. There is no console fallback, because a one-time
# code is a complete sign-in credential for whatever number was typed into an
# unauthenticated endpoint — printing one to a console makes anybody who can
# read that console able to sign in as anyone whose number they can spell.
#
# With Twilio unconfigured, phone sign-in is switched OFF rather than degraded:
# `GET /api/v1/auth/methods` leaves it out, the app draws no Phone button, and
# `POST /auth/phone/start` refuses with `otp_unavailable` before a code is
# generated at all.

# Country code assumed for a number typed without one, e.g. +44. Empty means
# "insist on a country code", which is the safe default — guessing a region
# would mean texting somebody else's phone a code that signs in as them.
DEFAULT_PHONE_COUNTRY_CODE=

# The limits, which are what actually make a six-digit code safe. Raising these
# weakens sign-in — they are not tuning knobs.
OTP_LENGTH=6
OTP_TTL_MINUTES=5
OTP_MAX_ATTEMPTS=5
OTP_SENDS_PER_HOUR=5
OTP_RESEND_SECONDS=30

# Twilio. Prefer TWILIO_MESSAGING_SERVICE_SID over TWILIO_FROM where you have
# one: it handles sender selection and regional compliance for you.
# TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
# TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
# TWILIO_FROM=+15550000000
# TWILIO_MESSAGING_SERVICE_SID=

# ===========================================================================
# Signing in with Google, Apple, Facebook and GitHub
# ===========================================================================
# EVERY SECRET BELOW IS A SERVER SECRET AND MUST NEVER REACH THE APP.
#
# The app cannot do these flows itself, because doing them needs a client
# secret, and a client secret compiled into an APK is a client secret published
# to everyone who installs it. So the app asks this deployment to start a flow,
# the browser goes to the provider, the provider comes back HERE, and this code
# does the exchange. What crosses back to the app is a single-use AURIX grant —
# never a provider token, never a secret.
#
# Leave a provider's keys empty and it is simply not offered.

# ---- Required for ALL FOUR providers --------------------------------------
# This deployment's public origin — the address the PROVIDER redirects a
# browser to. Must be reachable from the public internet and https in
# production, and the callback path must be registered in each provider's
# console verbatim:
#
#     {PUBLIC_API_URL}/api/v1/auth/oauth/{google|apple|facebook|github}/callback
#
# ON VERCEL THIS CHANGES. Moving from the old server's origin to the Vercel one
# changes all four callback URLs, and each must be re-registered by hand.
# Apple additionally requires domain verification. This cannot be automated and
# is the longest-lead item in the migration — start it early.
# PUBLIC_API_URL=https://aurix-api.vercel.app

# Where this deployment may hand the finished grant to. An exact-match
# allow-list, comma separated, and it is load bearing: the last hop of the flow
# puts a one-time credential in a URL, so without a list `?redirect_uri=` would
# make this API post that credential wherever it was asked to.
#
# Must match AURIX_LOGIN_REDIRECT_URI in the app's .env.
OAUTH_APP_REDIRECTS=aurix://login-callback

# How long a browser transaction, and the grant it produces, stay valid.
OAUTH_STATE_MINUTES=10
OAUTH_GRANT_MINUTES=10

# ---- Google ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Cloud console -> APIs & Services -> Credentials -> OAuth client ID ->
# Web application (not Android/iOS: the exchange happens here, so this is a
# confidential client even though the app is a mobile one).
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ---- Apple ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Apple has no client *secret*. It has a signing key, and the secret is a
# short-lived ES256 JWT minted from it on every exchange — hence four values.
#
#   APPLE_SERVICES_ID  a **Services ID** (not the App ID), with Sign in with
#                      Apple enabled, your domain verified, and the callback
#                      URL added to "Return URLs".
#   APPLE_TEAM_ID      Membership -> Team ID.
#   APPLE_KEY_ID       Keys -> the Sign in with Apple key.
#   APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY  the whole body of the .p8, BEGIN/END lines included,
#                      with every newline written as a literal backslash-n.
# APPLE_SERVICES_ID=com.example.aurix.web
# APPLE_TEAM_ID=
# APPLE_KEY_ID=
# APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY=

# ---- Facebook -------------------------------------------------------------
# developers.facebook.com -> your app -> Facebook Login -> Settings, with the
# callback URL under "Valid OAuth Redirect URIs".
# FACEBOOK_APP_ID=
# FACEBOOK_APP_SECRET=

# ---- GitHub ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Settings -> Developer settings -> OAuth Apps. Use an **OAuth App**, not a
# GitHub App: the code does not send PKCE for GitHub, because OAuth Apps do not
# implement it and sending the parameters makes the exchange fail.
# GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
# GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ===========================================================================
# Music providers — playlist import
# ===========================================================================
#
# Separate from the sign-in providers above, and worth understanding why: those
# answer "who is this person?" and produce an AURIX session. These answer "may
# AURIX read this person's playlists?" and produce a stored provider token used
# for nothing else.
#
# Both callbacks are {PUBLIC_API_URL}/api/v1/music/connections/{provider}/callback
# and must be registered with the provider exactly.
#
# The app redirect the browser finishes at is taken from OAUTH_APP_REDIRECTS
# above — the same allow-list the sign-in flow uses. No extra entry is needed.

# ---- Spotify --------------------------------------------------------------
# developer.spotify.com/dashboard -> your app -> Settings.
#
# **The secret belongs here and nowhere near the Flutter app.** mobile/.env
# holds SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID only, for the in-app PKCE flow used by playback;
# there is nowhere to hide a secret in a mobile binary, and with the import
# behind this API there is no longer any need to.
#
# Redirect URI to register:
#   {PUBLIC_API_URL}/api/v1/music/connections/spotify/callback
#
# Scopes AURIX requests: playlist-read-private, playlist-read-collaborative,
# user-read-private. Read-only, and no playback scope — this connection imports
# track lists and nothing else.
#
# WHAT THIS CANNOT DO, whatever you configure:
#   Since Spotify's February 2026 changes (enforced 9 March 2026),
#   GET /playlists/{id}/items is served only to a playlist's **owner or a
#   collaborator** and answers 403 to everyone else — while GET /playlists/{id}
#   still answers 200 with metadata for any playlist. So AURIX can import a
#   playlist the connected account owns or collaborates on, and cannot import
#   anyone else's, including Spotify's own editorial playlists. That is a policy,
#   not a misconfiguration, and no credential changes it.
# SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID=
# SPOTIFY_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ---- YouTube / YouTube Music ----------------------------------------------
# console.cloud.google.com -> enable "YouTube Data API v3".
#
# Falls back to GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID / GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET when these are unset,
# because it is usually the same OAuth application as the Google sign-in button.
# Set them separately only when you want a distinct client.
#
# Redirect URI to register:
#   {PUBLIC_API_URL}/api/v1/music/connections/youtube/callback
#
# Scope: https://www.googleapis.com/auth/youtube.readonly
# YOUTUBE_CLIENT_ID=
# YOUTUBE_CLIENT_SECRET=
#
# An API key, for PUBLIC playlists only. With it, a public YouTube or YouTube
# Music playlist imports with no consent screen at all — which is the whole
# point of setting it. It cannot see a private or unlisted playlist; that is
# what the OAuth pair above is for.
# Credentials -> API key, restricted to the YouTube Data API v3.
# YOUTUBE_API_KEY=

# ---- Provider token encryption --------------------------------------------
# Encrypts the stored provider refresh tokens at rest (AES-256-GCM).
#
# A refresh token is a long-lived credential for somebody's Spotify or Google
# account and does not expire on its own, so a leaked backup containing them is
# materially worse than one containing AURIX sessions. Every other secret this
# database holds is hashed; this one cannot be, because the server has to read
# it back.
#
# Falls back to a value derived from JWT_SECRET when unset, so a deployment
# that sets nothing still encrypts. Rotating it invalidates every stored
# connection — every user presses Connect once more, which is recoverable and
# the right direction to fail in.
#
# Generate with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))"
# MUSIC_TOKEN_KEY=

# ===========================================================================
# Deliberately absent, compared with server/.env.example
# ===========================================================================
#   PORT, HOST          There is no listener. Next.js is invoked per request.

#   OTP_DEV_DELIVERY    Wrote one-time codes to a file on disk. Serverless has
#   OTP_DEV_FILE        no durable filesystem, and the sink was a development
#                       affordance that should not outlive the migration.
#   NODE_ENV            Set by Next.js and by Vercel. Do not set it by hand.
